[8] R15-2's initial-memory term B for matter present at t0 (outside the class; subtraction: Psi_1(inf) = 0, printed)
    (units tau = 1) Psi_1(inf) = int_0^inf psi = int t f = 4.98e-14
    s = 10 tau: psi(s) = -1.99e-02; Psi_1(s) - Psi_1(inf) = 2.77e-02
    s = 20 tau: psi(s) = -7.79e-06; Psi_1(s) - Psi_1(inf) = 9.11e-06
    s = 40 tau: psi(s) = -1.32e-13; Psi_1(s) - Psi_1(inf) = 1.43e-13
    => B decays like s^3 e^{-s/tau}; at tau <= 5 ps it is gone within ~ 1e-10 s of t0

[9] double-pulsar drag (subtraction: none)
    linear order: the self-force is the odd part of the body's own mean response (Lemma D, vR13-6:3-7); initial data
    add a body-independent homogeneous solution, so Gamma is state-independent (proved in report §3.5)
    bath-friction upper estimate (geometric cross-section pi (GM/c^2)^2, momentum transfer x4): rho = 1e+00 rho_L -> Gamma <= 3.2e-41 s^-1 vs 6.1e-21
    bath-friction upper estimate (geometric cross-section pi (GM/c^2)^2, momentum transfer x4): rho = 1e+03 rho_L -> Gamma <= 3.2e-38 s^-1 vs 6.1e-21

